<title>NORWAY: FORECAST-Skog 1996 profits seen down.</title>
<headline>FORECAST-Skog 1996 profits seen down.</headline>
<dateline>OSLO 1997-02-04</dateline>
<text>
<p>Norske Skogindustrier ASA, Norway's biggest forestry group, is expected to post 1996 pre-tax profits of about 1.60 billion crowns, down from 2.34 billion in 1995, according to eight analysts polled by Reuters.</p>
<p>Four said buy, two said hold and one said sell the share, which closed at 197.50 crowns on the Oslo Bourse on Tuesday. The remaining analyst gave no recommendation.</p>
<p>Most analysts said they saw an upturn in forestry in general this year, but few believed in major price increases.</p>
<p>Most said they had downgraded the EPS estimate for 1997 and 1998 because of the strong Norwegian crown. The company has largely hedged against currency swings this year, but the full effect of a potentially strong crown in the longer term would show next year.</p>
<p>Norske Skog will present the annual result on February 13.</p>
